{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793619612,"nonce":"C9eDIlpc5ynM+Q44rMqjiJ6IbQo=","title":"Bound writes and CSS transitions: render props, and rule matching per write","message":"Bound layout writes now go through RenderTree::eased_layout_write, which starts the transition the node's rules declare (needed for cn::progress easing by rule). Two gaps remain:\n\n1. Bound render writes (background, color, opacity, transform via bind_*) still land without starting a transition, unlike CSS, where any computed-style change transitions. The render write path in apply_partial_property_updates writes props directly.\n\n2. eased_layout_write calls matching_transition for every bound layout write, which walks every complex rule and matches it against the node. A slider or gauge drag makes a couple of these per pointer move. Not measured as a problem yet; a per-node cache of the matched transition, invalidated when the node's classes, attributes or the stylesheet change, would make it a lookup.\n\nDoing (1) needs (2) first, since bound render writes are far more frequent (text colours, computed fills).","files":null}]}